    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 102045 CCA\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n126 PM PDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n*  The warming trend peaks Today with an Extreme Heat Warning in\n   effect. Temperatures cool into and through the weekend.\n\n*  Spotty storm activity will be possible in the Sierra Nevada and \n   higher elevations of eastern San Bernardino today. \n\n*  Breezy south-southwesterly wind gusts expected across the region \n   Friday through the weekend. With reduced humidites leading to \n   fire weather concerns.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through Wednesday\n\nTemperatures and HeatRisk peak today as afternoon highs look to peak \naround 5 degrees above average with most of the desert valleys \nranging from the upper 90s to around 105 degrees. Areas to the south \nof Lake Havasu will exceed 110 and around Death Valley looks to \nclimb above 115 degrees. As such, an Extreme Heat Warning is in \naffect until tonight for Death Valley, the Las Vegas Valley, Pahrump \nValley, Moapa Valley, Colorado River Valley, southern Mohave County, \nand southeastern San Bernardino County. The overnight hours will \noffer some relief, but lows will still range 5 to 10 degrees above \naverage. The upper level pattern begins to push a trough into the \narea from the Northwest, pushing the high pressure east, and \nallowing for a cooling trend starting Friday. \n\nWeak convection has moved through southern portions of the CWA \nearlier in the day with spotty shower activity moving through Sierra \nNevada near Bishop this after. So far, these showers have been on \nthe weaker side with some brief lightning activity, but don't look \nto be impactful.\n\nThe late week pattern change will increase the pressure gradient \nfrom the high pressure to the east to the building trough to the \nnorthwest and result in increasing winds. Friday afternoon will \noffer breezy to gusty winds that look to ease slightly overnight. \nWinds look to become even stronger for  Saturday with widespread \ngusts above 30 mph possible and above 40 mph possible in higher \nterrain and favored gaps and passes. This coupled with very dry \nconditions has increase fire weather concerns, with a Fire Weather \nWatch in effect for Nye, Esmeralda, and portions of Lincoln and \nClark counties for Saturday. Winds look to reduce for Sunday but \nremain breezy. Longer term models are starting to favor breezy winds \npeaking around 25 mph each afternoon into the first half of next \nweek.\n\n\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Wind \ndirections follow typical daily directional patterns, favoring an \neast to southeast direction with speeds under 10 knots. By the late \nafternoon, winds veer to the southwest and increase to over 10 \nknots, weakening overnight. Mid and high clouds filter through the \narea with little impact on aviation. Temperatures exceed 100 degrees \nbetween 19Z and 04Z. Some isolated thunderstorms could impact the \nDaggett corridor this afternoon.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Winds generally follow \ntypical daily directional patterns throughout the TAF period. \nSouthwesterly winds around 10 knots late this afternoon in the Las \nVegas Valley. At the same time, westerly winds at KDAG with gusts to \naround 25 knots. Isolated showers and thunderstorms develop along \nthe eastern Sierra this afternoon, some of which may reach the KBIH \nterminal and other locations in Owens Valley, potentially bringing \ngusty and erratic winds. Some storms are also possible in southern \nSan Bernardino County, but should remain east of KDAG. Outside of \nstorms, VFR conditions prevail with cloud bases at or above 12kft.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...A Fire Weather Watch is in effect from 11AM Saturday \nthrough midnight that night for Nye, Esmeralda, and portions of \nLincoln and Clark counties. Concerns include RH ranging from 5 to \n15% while winds begin to build above 25 mph and gusts approaching 40 \nmph. Winds will last deeper into that night, but humidities will \nbegin to have some recovery by the late that night.\n\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Murdock\nAVIATION...Gorelow\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
